(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-3-pharm-e3-antiarrhythmic-vignettes"]={"title":"Antiarrhythmic Drugs — Clinical Vignettes 1","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 3","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 62-year-old woman on amiodarone for a year reports fatigue, weight gain and feeling cold all the time. Which listed toxicity best fits?","choices":["Hypothyroidism","Hyperthyroidism","Hepatic dysfunction","Peripheral neuropathy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Amiodarone is an analog of thyroid hormone, and its thyroid abnormalities run in both directions; fatigue, weight gain and cold intolerance fit hypothyroidism. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A 28-year-old woman has sudden palpitations; her electrocardiogram shows a regular narrow-complex tachycardia, diagnosed as paroxysmal supraventricular tachycardia. Vagal maneuvers fail. Which drug is listed to terminate it?","choices":["Lidocaine","Adenosine","Magnesium sulfate","Dofetilide"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Adenosine is listed for paroxysmal supraventricular tachycardia; it hyperpolarizes cells and may stop the heart for 2 to 3 seconds. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"A 74-year-old man with severely reduced kidney function has atrial fibrillation. Which rhythm drug is most affected by his kidneys, being mainly eliminated unchanged there?","choices":["Dofetilide","Verapamil","Lidocaine","Procainamide"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Dofetilide is mainly eliminated unchanged in the kidneys. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"A 34-year-old man is about to receive adenosine for paroxysmal supraventricular tachycardia. Which effects should he be told to expect?","choices":["Ringing ears and dizziness","Flushing and chest burning","Drowsiness and twitching","Sedation and fatigue"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Adenosine's listed toxicities are flushing (20%), shortness of breath and burning in the chest; the heart may also pause briefly. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"A 55-year-old man taking propafenone develops a sore throat and fever, and his blood count shows almost no neutrophils. Which listed adverse effect is this?","choices":["Thrombocytopenia","Lupus-like syndrome","Agranulocytosis","Bronchospasm"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Propafenone's toxicity list includes agranulocytosis, anemia and thrombocytopenia. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man had a myocardial infarction two years ago and now has frequent premature ventricular contractions. Which drug should be avoided because it raises mortality after a myocardial infarction?","choices":["Propranolol","Metoprolol","Amiodarone","Flecainide"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Flecainide's toxicity list includes increased mortality after a myocardial infarction, and it is used only without significant structural heart damage. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"A 78-year-old woman with atrial fibrillation takes digoxin, and amiodarone was added two weeks ago. She now has anorexia, nausea, confusion and visual disturbances. Which is the most likely cause?","choices":["Amiodarone neurotoxicity","Digoxin toxicity","Amiodarone thyroid dysfunction","Cinchonism"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Amiodarone increases digoxin, and digoxin toxicity lists nausea, vomiting, diarrhea and anorexia with confusion, delirium, headache, seizures and visual disturbances. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"A 63-year-old man with cirrhosis has paroxysmal supraventricular tachycardia, and verapamil is considered. Why is caution needed?","choices":["It undergoes hepatic metabolism","It is cleared unchanged by the kidneys","It causes pulmonary fibrosis","It is acetylated to an active metabolite"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Verapamil and diltiazem undergo hepatic metabolism, so caution is advised in liver damage. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"A 57-year-old woman with asthma is started on propranolol for a supraventricular arrhythmia. Two days later she is wheezing. Which property of propranolol explains this?","choices":["It is beta-1 selective","It is nonselective","It blocks potassium channels","It is membrane stabilizing"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Propranolol is a nonselective beta blocker, and bronchospasm is on its toxicity list. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 69-year-old woman with atrial fibrillation is started on dofetilide to restore normal rhythm. Which finding needs the closest watch?","choices":["Corneal deposits","Platelet count","Blue-gray skin","QT prolongation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Dofetilide's listed adverse effects are marked QT prolongation and torsades de pointes (1 to 3%). — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"A 62-year-old man in the coronary unit with an acute myocardial infarction has frequent ventricular premature beats arising from ischemic tissue. Which class I drug works best on sodium channels in ischemic tissue?","choices":["Flecainide","Quinidine","Lidocaine","Disopyramide"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Lidocaine (class IB) is given intravenously and is most effective on sodium channels in ischemic tissue, with less effect on normal tissue. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"A 72-year-old man in the intensive care unit after surgery has a supraventricular tachycardia. The team wants an intravenous beta blocker whose effect wears off quickly. Which drug fits?","choices":["Propranolol","Metoprolol","Sotalol","Esmolol"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Esmolol is the intravenous, short-acting beta blocker. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman takes quinidine to maintain rhythm after atrial flutter. She now reports ringing in her ears, dizziness and blurred vision. Which listed toxicity explains her symptoms?","choices":["Lupus-like syndrome","Hypersensitivity reaction","Thyroid dysfunction","Cinchonism"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Cinchonism (tinnitus, dizziness and blurred vision) is on quinidine's toxicity list; quinidine is an isomer of quinine. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man with glaucoma needs a class IA drug for ventricular tachycardia. Which class IA drug is contraindicated for him?","choices":["Procainamide","Quinidine","Disopyramide","Lidocaine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Disopyramide has more anticholinergic action than quinidine, and glaucoma is on its contraindication list with heart block and hypersensitivity. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man has taken amiodarone for two years. He now has a dry cough, worsening breathlessness and fine crackles at both lung bases. Which listed toxicity is most likely?","choices":["Bronchospasm","Respiratory paralysis","Pulmonary fibrosis","Heart failure"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Pneumonitis and pulmonary fibrosis are amiodarone's potentially fatal toxicity, building up on long-term use. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 33"}]};
